<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Florida</h1><p class="st-meta">1565 CE – 1821 CE · capital St. Augustine</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's oldest North American colony, centred on St Augustine, the oldest continuously inhabited European-founded city in the United States.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> named La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in 1513, but several attempts at settlement failed. When French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> built <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-florida">Fort Caroline</a> in French Florida, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a>, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-augustine-florida">St Augustine</a> in 1565 and destroyed the French colony. Florida became a military frontier guarding the route of the Spanish treasure fleets through the Bahama Channel.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> was a poor garrison colony, supported by a royal subsidy from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> built a chain of missions among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guale">Guale</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/apalachee-chiefdom">Apalachee</a>, who supplied food and labour. The stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castillo-de-san-marcos">Castillo de San Marcos</a>, begun in 1672, resisted English sieges in 1702 and 1740, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> was founded in 1698. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-mose">Fort Mose</a>, established in 1738 for people escaping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Carolina</a>, was the first legally free Black town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>English raids wrecked the missions after 1702. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1763, when it was split into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-east-florida">Province of East Florida</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-west-florida">Province of West Florida</a>, regained it in 1783, and sold it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Adams-Onís Treaty</a> of 1819, effective in 1821.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-florida.jpg" alt="Spanish Florida" width="320" height="385"><figcaption>Image: Henry Charles Carey, Isaac Lea, Fielding Lucas (Life time: October 13, 1879; December 8, 1886; March 12, 1854) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1822_Geographical,_Statistical,_and_Historical_Map_of_Florida_by_Henry_Charles_Carey,_Isaac_Lea_and_Fielding_Lucas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1565 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St. Augustine</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pedro Menéndez de Avilés, Manuel de Montiano</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>La Florida</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1693&amp;focus=spanish-florida" aria-label="Spanish Florida on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox=